#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498637} :activity/id #uuid "8ad2254c-ea29-5a48-be63-2aea3b2e0010" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048786 :topic/id #uuid "bea5c820-dd0e-5c3b-936d-578694b34907" :topic/math-academy-id 4171} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c52bfe3-c132-51f9-9f17-59b76eb15a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282471} {:answer/id #uuid "af8eb9d5-df46-5f33-9b42-1dcec1ba4a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283206} {:answer/id #uuid "c8221ae2-715d-58b9-bc75-011e8e34610d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283439} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9a049b-ce44-5f95-85cb-8df2f5edcf7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283460} {:answer/id #uuid "e5217236-8877-51f1-a405-45114581cfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283206} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e3460f-903c-51fc-a33d-e048585ab148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "476ff359-d053-5b12-8e13-351e9dc83e70" :question/math-academy-id "q-164859" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(0,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2,0) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-5+\\frac{1}{2}(-6) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "28c1a616-52f4-5075-8df8-9fb2aea0d0f9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0488d7ff-b23d-57ca-ac1f-3b003b1d4cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278628} {:answer/id #uuid "19abce14-7fbc-5539-b049-f6588065cc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278820} {:answer/id #uuid "1b20a70e-e52e-59a8-a425-efc1714f3b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278843} {:answer/id #uuid "4be49a5e-04bf-5a10-ba8f-0ff99825ca37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279271} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c391e8-39ad-5fb2-9766-ec1eb8cec68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278820} :answer-field/id #uuid "11825cc6-3e2c-51ee-8c64-1a71cc1b22e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7670cca-4082-532e-925a-af41e094c0c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163122" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & -x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "754b4ab2-0c61-518a-8fe4-5c04a479e6df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "600bf2e3-6ccc-5e19-9c6d-a11e065651ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282503} {:answer/id #uuid "88308563-47ab-55ac-b349-b96b91b0c069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282861} {:answer/id #uuid "90dcefcb-039e-53fd-ad50-070bdaeaeafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282943} {:answer/id #uuid "e899ed7d-bdce-5f3e-8857-e9c5204e70b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283714} {:answer/id #uuid "f88eee42-846c-5f36-8d70-74d6ce1e4b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282943} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bee6918-a74e-5833-a9b9-7d8fc82fe9ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f42addea-156e-5dc2-b6d9-50bdb56ae67d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164827" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =2, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =2, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & ≈2+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7ae3c717-af7e-5f78-a5cd-4c69f63eb716" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a5e6d9-c4fd-57bb-8147-cf98861b9d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367813} {:answer/id #uuid "1719c348-9c4f-5f66-9ae0-a4eafaef1b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367814} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f81270-21c7-5914-a3c9-3982421e3eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367815} {:answer/id #uuid "f5eb386a-91a6-5494-94a1-33f62862e3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367816} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb293b3-3530-5187-923a-dc9dda0e6dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367817} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4e6984-cf2e-55cb-9a10-debfbaccb6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cdc26b29-0619-5b64-aa82-450f2b706603" :question/math-academy-id "q-163106" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y} \\\\ -{e}^{x-y} & {e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-1)+(-1)+(x-1)=2x-3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9284e89b-e849-5c80-93e0-fd7a47bc583b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d4caa4-4beb-5879-be0e-2ecd7839fde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y+6e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278675} {:answer/id #uuid "673f89a5-8bcd-52d1-a51d-1e9b6862c990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279527} {:answer/id #uuid "8518450c-6a7d-5908-8248-9d4d5e973d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279786} {:answer/id #uuid "e00a9b9b-e6c8-5944-9c02-0f2375033954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y+10e-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280570} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e66d77-2537-5e67-9679-68844748ea83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+10e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280737}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280570} :answer-field/id #uuid "55491170-a6a3-5d0f-aeb5-a0ffe8175fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "61a1651c-7c64-5ebe-93b5-ec0cfeb0b0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163144" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{3x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3x-y} & -{e}^{3x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,2) & =[\\begin{aligned}3e & -e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-y} & -3{e}^{3x-y} \\\\ -3{e}^{3x-y} & {e}^{3x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,2) & =[\\begin{aligned}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈e+[\\begin{aligned}3e & -e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\ne+3e+(y-2)+(y-2)+9e+(-3e)=2y+10e-4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a67dda22-3935-5689-915a-87b3021a1579" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498634}}] :activity/title "Second-Degree Taylor Polynomials of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497791}]}
